{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggzaKUoDYSKuxjVyai6Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For [Co(ox)₃]³⁻, **Dq ≈ 1856 cm⁻¹** (10Dq ≈ 18 557 cm⁻¹), **B ≈ 432 cm⁻¹** and **C ≈ 1946 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Approach.** Co(III) oxalate is a low-spin d⁶ ion with a ¹A₁g ground state (t₂g⁶). The two spin-allowed bands come from the t₂g⁵e_g¹ configuration. I used the standard strong-field expressions for these transitions:\n\n- ¹A₁g → ¹T₁g: E₁ = 10Dq − C\n- ¹A₁g → ¹T₂g: E₂ = 10Dq + 16B − C\n\nThese are first-order expressions. They leave out configuration interaction with other configurations, which is the usual textbook approximation for this kind of problem.\n\n**Step 1 – Find B.** Subtracting the two band energies removes both Dq and C:\n\nE₂ − E₁ = 16B\n23 529 − 16 611 = 6 918 cm⁻¹\nB = 6 918 / 16 ≈ **432 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Step 2 – Find C.** With C/B = 4.5:\n\nC = 4.5 × 432.4 ≈ **1 946 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Step 3 – Find Dq.** From the first band:\n\n10Dq = E₁ + C = 16 611 + 1 946 ≈ 18 557 cm⁻¹\nDq ≈ **1 856 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Check.** B ≈ 432 cm⁻¹ is about 39 % of the free-ion Co³⁺ value (roughly 1 100 cm⁻¹). That is a nephelauxetic ratio β ≈ 0.39, which fits a strongly covalent low-spin Co(III) complex.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1855.7, \"B\": 432.4, \"C\": 1945.7, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Low-spin d6 strong-field expressions 10Dq-C and 10Dq+16B-C with C=4.5B give B, C and Dq.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":820,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":820,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e9331ee1-f368-49a3-9118-5dd576b962d9","uuid":"2990c31f-5fd1-4cc2-8aeb-b336149870eb","timestamp":"2026-11-04T0